### 1. The "Quick Fix" (Cleaner & Safer)
The original code has a logic error in the recursive calls. It sorts the range `[low, j]` and `[i, high]`. However, `i` and `j` are the indices *after* the swap loop finished. The pivot is actually located at index `j` (or `i-1` depending on implementation).

The most common bug in this specific implementation is that the recursive calls might overlap or skip elements if `i` and `j` cross incorrectly.

Here is the corrected, standard version:

```java
public class QuickSort {
    public static void quickSort(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
        if (arr == null || arr.length == 0 || low >= high) {
            return;
        }

        // 1. Pivot Selection: Middle is safer than First/Last for sorted data
        int pivotIndex = low + (high - low) / 2;
        int pivot = arr[pivotIndex];

        // 2. Partitioning
        int i = low;
        int j = high;

        while (i <= j) {
            // Move i forward while element is smaller than pivot
            while (arr[i] < pivot) {
                i++;
            }
            // Move j backward while element is larger than pivot
            while (arr[j] > pivot) {
                j--;
            }

            // Swap if pointers haven't crossed
            if (i <= j) {
                int temp = arr[i];
                arr[i] = arr[j];
                arr[j] = temp;
                i++;
                j--;
            }
        }

        // 3. Recursive Calls
        // Note: The pivot is now at index 'j' (or 'i-1'). 
        // We sort the left part (low to j) and right part (i to high).
        if (low < j) {
            quickSort(arr, low, j);
        }
        if (i < high) {
            quickSort(arr, i, high);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {25, 2, 8, 60, 3, 1, 90};
        quickSort(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);

        for (int i : arr) {
            System.out.print(i + " ");
        }
    }
}
```

---

### 2. The "Optimized" Version (Randomized Pivot)
The biggest performance killer in QuickSort is **worst-case time complexity** ($O(n^2)$), which happens if the array is already sorted and you always pick the first or middle element as the pivot.

**Efficiency Gain:** Randomizing the pivot ensures that even on sorted data, the split is balanced, guaranteeing an average $O(n \log n)$ performance.

